,279,2097,429,279,2629,315,279,25941,304,264,21495,374,17767,220,16,23,15,24884,43298,1124,982,256,1124,9640,256,1124,4044,19360,284,220,16,23,15,24884,43298,481,1124,4044,425,1706,481,1124,4044,425,5049,284,220,16,23,15,24884,43298,481,220,18,20,24884,43298,481,220,18,20,24884,43298,284,220,16,16,15,24884,43298,624,256,1124,21675,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,23526,504,279,74003,13,9258,1172,279,41171,13,3155,537,17024,279,6291,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nIn isosceles triangle $ABC$, angle $BAC$ and angle $BCA$ measure 35 degrees. What is the measure of angle $CDA$? [asy]\nimport olympiad; import geometry; size(125); defaultpen(linewidth(0.8));\ndraw(circle((0,0),1));\ndot(\"$F$\",(0,0),dir(-90));\npair A = dir(160), B = dir(110), C = dir(40), D = dir(10);\ndraw(A--D--C--A--B--C);\nlabel(\"$A$\",A,W); label(\"$B$\",B,N); label(\"$C$\",C,E); label(\"$D$\",D,E);\n[/asy]\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo solve for the measure of angle \\( CDA \\) in the given isosceles triangle \\( ABC \\), we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Determine the measure of angle \\( ABC \\):**\n   Since \\( \\triangle ABC \\) is isosceles with \\( \\angle BAC = \\angle BCA = 35^\\circ \\), we can find \\( \\angle ABC \\) by using the fact that the sum of the angles in a triangle is \\( 180^\\circ \\):\n   \\[\n   \\angle ABC = 180^\\circ - \\angle BAC - \\angle BCA = 180^\\circ - 35^\\circ - 35^\\circ = 110^\\circ.\n   \\]\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"20938f681deb7b633b8dd3d9f2f54feaa6c0e441010cdcdd8fac5ceac15178d4"},"seconds":12.20419692993164,"seed":747075883868926883,"stop":"eos","text":"2. **Identify the cyclic nature and use properties of cyclic quadrilaterals:**\n   Since points \\(A, B, C, D\\) lie on the circumference of the circle (as suggested by the diagram), \\(ABCD\\) is a cyclic quadrilateral. In a cyclic quadrilateral, the opposite angles are supplementary, meaning they add up to \\(180^\\circ\\). Therefore, \\( \\angle ABC + \\angle CDA = 180^\\circ \\).\n\n3. **Calculate the measure of angle \\( CDA \\):**\n   Using the supplementary property of opposite angles in a cyclic quadrilateral:\n   \\[\n   \\angle CDA = 180^\\circ - \\angle ABC = 180^\\circ - 110^\\circ = 70^\\circ.\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the measure of angle \\( CDA \\) is \\(\\boxed{70^\\circ}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{70^\\circ}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"2. **Identify the cyclic nature and use properties of cyclic quadrilaterals:**\n   Since points \\(A, B, C, D\\) lie on the circumference of the circle (as suggested by the diagram), \\(ABCD\\) is a cyclic quadrilateral. In a cyclic quadrilateral, the opposite angles are supplementary, meaning they add up to \\(180^\\circ\\). Therefore, \\( \\angle ABC + \\angle CDA = 180^\\circ \\).\n\n3. **Calculate the measure of angle \\( CDA \\):**\n   Using the supplementary property of opposite angles in a cyclic quadrilateral:\n   \\[\n   \\angle CDA = 180^\\circ - \\angle ABC = 180^\\circ - 110^\\circ = 70^\\circ.\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the measure of angle \\( CDA \\) is \\(\\boxed{70^\\circ}\\).","id":"geometry:469"}
